WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:05.080
I want you to imagine like a giant planetary scale seesaw.

00:05.240 --> 00:06.520
Okay, I'm picturing it.

00:06.520 --> 00:14.360
It's just a massive mechanism that has been rhythmically tilting back and forth for hundreds of thousands of years.

00:15.440 --> 00:19.260
And on each end of the seesaw rests the rain supply for half the globe.

00:19.940 --> 00:27.280
So when one side goes up, a massive monsoon drenches Asia, right, bringing life-giving water to billions of acres of land.

00:27.500 --> 00:29.600
Which is an almost unfathomable amount of water.

00:30.000 --> 00:30.760
Exactly.

00:31.600 --> 00:38.160
And then when the seesaw tilts the other way, that rain empties over South America, you know, feeding the Amazon basin.

00:39.000 --> 00:46.600
For eons, this alternating rhythm has pretty much fundamentally dictated where the water falls on Earth.

00:46.840 --> 00:48.980
It is a really striking image.

00:49.360 --> 00:56.280
And scientifically, it represents the foundational baseline of how we've understood the global climate system.

00:56.920 --> 00:59.000
I mean, we rely on that alternating rhythm.

00:59.000 --> 01:01.940
The opposition between the hemispheres isn't just a pattern.

01:02.120 --> 01:06.040
It is a fundamental expectation of atmospheric physics.

01:06.860 --> 01:06.920
Right.

01:07.020 --> 01:08.020
It's like a law of nature.

01:08.280 --> 01:10.180
A very comforting metronome.

01:10.660 --> 01:17.640
But now I want you to imagine what happens when you're watching this seesaw and suddenly the fulcrum just snaps.

01:17.980 --> 01:18.900
The whole mechanism breaks.

01:19.160 --> 01:19.340
Yeah.

01:19.340 --> 01:23.900
Instead of alternating, both sides of the seesaw plummet to the ground at the exact same time.

01:24.620 --> 01:29.460
The water just dries up on both sides of the planet simultaneously.

01:29.820 --> 01:33.260
Which completely defies the baseline expectation of the entire system.

01:33.260 --> 01:39.180
I mean, if the water isn't in Asia, and it isn't in South America, where did it go?

01:39.420 --> 01:39.860
Exactly.

01:40.660 --> 01:45.300
The physics of the atmosphere shouldn't allow for a synchronized failure of that magnitude.

01:45.680 --> 01:48.880
Or at least, you know, not according to our standard operating models.

01:48.880 --> 01:55.060
Okay, let's unpack this, because that broken seesaw is exactly the mystery we are diving into today.

01:55.260 --> 02:04.980
Our source material for this deep dive is a wonderfully dense, incredibly fascinating independent essay from October 2026 by an author named Flyxion.

02:05.260 --> 02:06.020
Yeah, it's a great piece.

02:06.020 --> 02:12.580
It's titled Regularity Without Invariance, The Limits of the Interhemispheric Monsoon Dipole.

02:13.160 --> 02:22.400
And what Flyxion is doing here is deeply analyzing a massive, groundbreaking 2026 climate study published by a team of researchers led by Olson.

02:22.640 --> 02:26.600
The Olson et al. study is just a monumental piece of paleoclimatology.

02:27.260 --> 02:35.080
They essentially built a continuous 450,000-year-old climate record using stalagmites from caves deep in Peru.

02:35.080 --> 02:36.260
Which is wild.

02:36.460 --> 02:37.260
It really is.

02:37.940 --> 02:44.600
Their goal was to prove that this global seesaw exists, but the data ended up doing something far more interesting.

02:45.040 --> 02:50.640
It showed exactly when, how, and why the seesaw periodically broke down over the last half million years.

02:50.820 --> 02:51.160
Yes.

02:51.560 --> 02:58.540
So our mission today, for you listening, is to unpack this ancient climate mystery hidden inside these Peruvian cave formations.

02:58.540 --> 03:08.220
We're going to explore why a seemingly permanent law of nature, the opposing weather patterns of the northern and southern hemispheres, periodically just stops working.

03:08.380 --> 03:09.740
It's quite the detective story.

03:09.900 --> 03:10.520
It really is.

03:11.040 --> 03:16.100
We're going to look at the mathematical realities of global rain, the hidden power of ocean currents,

03:16.100 --> 03:22.600
and what this ancient broken seesaw reveals about the absolute limits of predicting our own future climate.

03:22.600 --> 03:26.920
Well, before we can begin to diagnose how the global climate system broke,

03:27.580 --> 03:32.120
we really have to establish an airtight understanding of how it operates under normal conditions.

03:32.260 --> 03:32.360
Right.

03:32.440 --> 03:32.860
The baseline.

03:33.200 --> 03:33.560
Exactly.

03:34.340 --> 03:39.460
The baseline mechanics of this system are governed by celestial forces that are so powerful,

03:40.100 --> 03:42.940
they almost seem to operate outside of earthly physics entirely.

03:42.940 --> 03:48.540
The formal name for this baseline seesaw is the interhemispheric monsoon dipole.

03:49.300 --> 03:54.120
And Phloxion points out that for the vast majority of the past 450,000 years,

03:54.640 --> 03:59.940
the Asian summer monsoon and the South American summer monsoon have been in strict opposite phases.

04:00.140 --> 04:00.780
Total opposition.

04:01.000 --> 04:01.180
Right.

04:01.600 --> 04:03.700
Asia gets wet, South America gets dry.

04:04.440 --> 04:09.320
Then thousands of years pass, the orbital configuration of the Earth shifts, and the reverse happens.

04:09.960 --> 04:11.440
It's a massive pendulum.

04:11.440 --> 04:14.480
But I guess, what is actually pushing the pendulum?

04:14.980 --> 04:17.300
Well, it all comes down to Milankovic cycles.

04:18.180 --> 04:22.140
Specifically, the component of those cycles known as orbital precession.

04:22.460 --> 04:29.080
I always picture orbital precession like a spinning top that's just starting to lose its momentum.

04:29.800 --> 04:36.240
You know, it spins perfectly upright for a while, but then the axis starts to trace a slow, wide circle in the air.

04:36.700 --> 04:37.700
That's a great analogy.

04:37.700 --> 04:45.120
The Earth's axis wobbles in that exact same way, just on a massive 23,000-year timescale.

04:45.620 --> 04:49.600
Yeah, and that rotational wobble fundamentally alters the calendar date of perihelion.

04:50.280 --> 04:51.320
Perihelion being?

04:51.820 --> 04:56.300
The specific day of the year when the Earth is physically closest to the Sun in its elliptical orbit.

04:56.300 --> 05:04.680
So, as the axis slowly sweeps through that 23,000-year circle, the seasonal distribution of incoming solar radiation changes.

05:05.220 --> 05:07.480
And we call this incoming radiation insulation.

05:07.480 --> 05:17.980
So, to be clear, for anyone listening who might be conflating this with global warming, it's not that the Earth is receiving more total heat from the Sun over the course of a full year.

05:18.140 --> 05:18.700
No, not at all.

05:18.960 --> 05:22.360
The total energy budget of the planet remains relatively stable.

05:23.040 --> 05:30.060
It's just that the delivery schedule of that heat is being rerouted, depending on the hemisphere and the season.

05:30.060 --> 05:37.500
Exactly. The annual mean radiation at any given latitude barely fluctuates, but the seasonal intensity changes dramatically.

05:38.260 --> 05:42.500
And in the tropics and subtropics, that seasonal distribution dictates everything.

05:42.740 --> 05:43.420
It's the engine.

05:43.880 --> 05:44.160
Right.

05:44.860 --> 05:50.000
When this precession cycle causes the northern hemisphere to receive intensely concentrated summer sun,

05:50.720 --> 05:56.820
the vast landmasses of the north, especially across Asia, warm up much faster than the surrounding global oceans.

05:56.820 --> 06:00.140
Because rock and soil have a lower heat capacity than water, right?

06:00.560 --> 06:05.460
The continent just bakes, while the ocean sort of absorbs the heat slowly.

06:05.920 --> 06:06.380
Precisely.

06:07.040 --> 06:13.660
This differential heating creates a profound temperature contrast between the hot land surface and the cooler air offshore.

06:13.940 --> 06:15.440
And that contrast drives the wind.

06:15.820 --> 06:16.060
Yes.

06:16.800 --> 06:25.080
This thermal engine pulls the intratropical convergence zone, the ITCZ, which is the main belt of rainfall that circles the equator.

06:25.080 --> 06:26.600
It pulls it northward.

06:27.120 --> 06:31.800
It drags the deep, moisture-laden tropical monsoon rains right over Asia.

06:32.500 --> 06:38.700
It really is like a slow, deeply predictable metronome that's been dictating the rhythm of global weather for eons.

06:39.340 --> 06:39.660
Tick.

06:40.040 --> 06:42.220
The northern hemisphere bakes in the summer sun.

06:42.540 --> 06:43.300
Asia gets drenched.

06:43.880 --> 06:44.260
Talk.

06:44.700 --> 06:48.760
11,500 years later, the axis has wobbled to the other side.

06:49.100 --> 06:51.500
The southern hemisphere gets the intense summer sun.

06:51.820 --> 06:53.040
And South America gets drenched.

06:53.040 --> 06:55.640
It's beautiful in its simplicity.

06:56.420 --> 06:56.800
It is.

06:57.220 --> 07:01.720
And Flyxion makes it clear that this isn't just a neat theoretical model on a chalkboard.

07:02.340 --> 07:08.160
The Olsen et al. study found an incredibly strong mathematical correlation in the physical record to back this up.

07:08.380 --> 07:08.640
They did.

07:08.920 --> 07:19.100
They calculated a correlation coefficient of r equals 0.67 between their reconstructed dipole index and the cross-equatorial insulation difference.

07:19.280 --> 07:20.480
Which is huge, right?

07:20.980 --> 07:21.520
Massive.

07:22.080 --> 07:35.160
To contextualize that number, you know, in the incredibly noisy, chaotic world of paleoclimatology, finding an r value of 0.67 sustained over a 450,000-year timeline is striking.

07:35.360 --> 07:35.600
Right.

07:35.600 --> 07:42.320
If we assume a linear relationship, squaring that value gives us an r squared of about 0.45.

07:42.820 --> 07:52.920
This means that celestial orbital forcing alone accounts for roughly 45% of the variance in this massive, chaotic, global monsoon system.

07:52.920 --> 08:05.720
Which means the astronomical theory correctly predicts the sign of the rain, meaning whether a hemisphere is wet or dry, it predicts the 23,000-year frequency perfectly, and it predicts the exact timing of the shifts.

08:06.100 --> 08:09.480
That is just a massive triumph for orbital climate theory.

08:09.480 --> 08:10.440
It really is.

08:10.820 --> 08:14.760
But what's fascinating here is a vital distinction Flipsian introduces.

08:15.380 --> 08:20.600
We have to separate astronomical forcing from atmospheric response.

08:20.980 --> 08:22.120
Okay, break that down.

08:22.520 --> 08:28.060
Well, the insulation anomaly, that orbital wobble we're talking about, is celestial mechanics.

08:28.560 --> 08:30.240
It is gravity and geometry.

08:30.980 --> 08:35.520
We can calculate it backward for millions of years with absolute pinpoint precision.

08:35.640 --> 08:36.240
Like clockwork.

08:36.240 --> 08:41.360
But incoming solar radiation does not spontaneously condense into rain droplets.

08:42.200 --> 08:42.280
Right.

08:42.840 --> 08:46.720
The sun provides the energy, but the Earth has to do the actual work of making the weather.

08:46.980 --> 08:47.360
Exactly.

08:48.320 --> 08:53.180
The orbital configuration merely sets the boundary conditions for the energy supplied to the top of the atmosphere.

08:54.180 --> 09:04.780
The Earth's climate system then has to absorb that energy, translate it into sensible and latent heat, and push it through an incredibly messy, complicated chain of intermediate processes.

09:05.360 --> 09:06.080
Like what?

09:06.240 --> 09:15.380
You have ocean heat transport, atmospheric circulation cells, localized convection, mountain topography, cloud albedo feedback loops.

09:16.420 --> 09:22.200
So the celestial math is perfect, but the atmospheric response is subject to the chaotic reality of fluid dynamics.

09:22.200 --> 09:22.660
Yes.

09:22.660 --> 09:30.340
So when we casually say the Earth's wobble causes the monsoon, we are employing a massive shorthand.

09:30.340 --> 09:37.000
We're compressing thousands of chaotic intervening steps into one very clean, easily digestible narrative.

09:37.000 --> 09:45.700
And as Flyxion argues throughout the essay, relying too heavily on that compressed narrative is exactly what blinds us to the truth of what happens when the regularity fails.

09:46.140 --> 09:55.760
Because to actually prove that the celestial seesaw governs earthly rain, and to catch the moments where it breaks, scientists couldn't just look at astronomical models on a computer.

09:55.760 --> 09:57.180
No, they needed hard data.

09:57.600 --> 09:57.760
Right.

09:57.920 --> 10:02.820
They needed a pristine, physical, localized record of ancient weather, locked in stone.

10:03.180 --> 10:06.040
Which brings us to the time machine part of this deep dive.

10:06.040 --> 10:13.020
I am endlessly fascinated by the sheer physical effort and ingenuity required to extract paleoclimate data.

10:13.260 --> 10:14.000
It's grueling work.

10:14.640 --> 10:22.340
The Olsen et al. study didn't rely on the usual suspects, like drilling ice cores in Antarctica or pulling up ocean sediment in the Atlantic.

10:22.680 --> 10:25.120
They built their record out of stalagmites.

10:25.400 --> 10:27.280
30 stalagmites in total.

10:27.860 --> 10:35.640
These were meticulously collected from three specific, highly isolated caves on the eastern flank of the Peruvian Andes, situated in the upper Amazon basin.

10:36.040 --> 10:37.580
And these caves have names, right?

10:37.760 --> 10:38.020
Yes.

10:38.020 --> 10:43.320
The caves are named Antipayaguna, Pakupahwain, and Huagapo.

10:43.920 --> 10:50.660
Just visualizing the logistics of hauling 30 fragile ancient rock formations out of cave systems high in the Andes is staggering.

10:51.100 --> 10:54.000
But the laboratory work they did on those rocks is even more intense.

10:54.520 --> 11:03.160
I mean, they extracted 13,939 separate oxygen isotope measurements from the calcite layers of these stalagmites.

11:03.360 --> 11:04.780
That is a lot of data points.

11:04.780 --> 11:10.120
And to figure out exactly when those layers were formed, they relied on uranium-thorium decay dating.

11:10.780 --> 11:16.360
Uranium-thorium, or UTH dating, is really the gold standard for this specific kind of terrestrial proxy.

11:17.080 --> 11:21.660
The mechanics of why it works so perfectly for cave formations are actually quite elegant.

11:22.320 --> 11:22.840
How so?

11:23.220 --> 11:25.060
Well, uranium is soluble in water.

11:25.600 --> 11:26.800
Thorium is not.

11:26.800 --> 11:33.320
As rainwater percolates through the soil and the limestone bedrock above the cave, it picks up trace amounts of uranium.

11:33.960 --> 11:38.300
But because thorium is insoluble, it gets left behind in the rock above.

11:38.300 --> 11:48.260
So when a drop of water finally falls from the ceiling of the cave and hits the floor to start forming the stalagmite, it contains a tiny bit of uranium, but absolutely zero thorium.

11:48.500 --> 11:48.960
Exactly.

11:49.700 --> 11:50.840
The clock starts at zero.

11:51.380 --> 11:51.740
Wow.

11:52.080 --> 11:55.200
The moment that calcite crystallizes, the system is sealed.

11:55.200 --> 12:05.800
Over thousands of years, the radioactive uranium-234 trapped in the stalagmite slowly decays into thorium-230 at a strictly known invariable rate.

12:06.300 --> 12:07.780
So it's literally a stopwatch.

12:08.080 --> 12:08.360
Yes.

12:08.980 --> 12:20.820
By simply measuring the ratio of uranium to thorium in a specific microscopic layer of the rock, scientists can calculate exactly how long ago that layer crystallized, down to an incredibly tight error margin.

12:20.820 --> 12:27.760
The Olsen team obtained 217 new UTH ages and incorporated 90 previously published ones.

12:28.260 --> 12:30.440
But they didn't just plot these dates on a simple graph, right?

12:30.780 --> 12:35.640
Flyxion mentions they ran all of this data through something called the B-Cron algorithm to build their timeline.

12:36.120 --> 12:39.720
B-Cron is a Bayesian age-depth modeling tool.

12:41.100 --> 12:45.060
In the real world, stalagmites don't grow at a perfectly uniform rate.

12:45.680 --> 12:47.800
Sometimes the cave is wetter and they grow faster.

12:48.200 --> 12:50.360
Sometimes it's drier and they barely grow at all.

12:50.360 --> 12:50.720
Right.

12:50.960 --> 13:01.840
If you only have, say, 50 firm dating points along a rock that represents 100,000 years, you have to interpolate the ages of all the layers in between.

13:02.140 --> 13:04.640
You have to guess the growth rate between the dots.

13:04.960 --> 13:05.200
Right.

13:05.980 --> 13:13.120
Rather than just drawing a straight line between the dots, B-Cron uses Markov chain Monte Carlo simulations.

13:14.020 --> 13:15.320
Okay, that sounds intense.

13:15.320 --> 13:26.480
It basically generates thousands of possible growth pathways that fit the data, assigning a mathematical probability to each one based on prior knowledge of how stalagmites accumulate.

13:26.480 --> 13:29.040
So, it's finding the most likely path.

13:29.460 --> 13:29.800
Exactly.

13:30.400 --> 13:43.540
The result is a continuous climate record stretching back 450 millennia with an average temporal resolution of just 50 years, complete with a rigorously quantified envelope of uncertainty.

13:43.540 --> 13:54.800
So, every 50 years, basically the span of a single human adult life, for the last 450,000 years, we have a data point for the weather in the Amazon basin.

13:54.800 --> 13:57.700
That is an absurd level of detail.

13:58.160 --> 13:59.900
It's unprecedented for this region.

14:00.340 --> 14:02.260
But we need to translate the chemistry here.

14:02.360 --> 14:08.060
How does the chemistry of a piece of cave rock tell you how hard it rained hundreds of thousands of years ago?

14:08.240 --> 14:13.860
This relies on the mechanics of moisture transport physics, specifically isotopic fractionation.

14:13.860 --> 14:21.760
We are looking at the ratio of oxygen-18 to oxygen-16 in the calcite, which is denoted as delta-18-0.

14:22.360 --> 14:24.240
Okay, let me make sure I've got this.

14:24.780 --> 14:29.960
Most oxygen is oxygen-16, meaning it has 8 protons and 8 neutrons.

14:30.560 --> 14:33.560
But oxygen-18 has 2 extra neutrons.

14:33.660 --> 14:35.800
It is physically a heavier isotope.

14:35.800 --> 14:36.360
Yes.

14:37.080 --> 14:45.120
And because it has those 2 extra neutrons, a water molecule containing oxygen-18 is thermodynamically slightly different.

14:45.840 --> 14:47.880
It requires more energy to evaporate.

14:48.340 --> 14:53.040
And critically, it condenses back into a liquid state more easily than lighter water.

14:53.720 --> 14:56.900
Okay, let's trace a cloud to see how this works in practice.

14:57.620 --> 15:02.660
Imagine a massive, wet air mass gathering moisture over the tropical Atlantic Ocean.

15:02.660 --> 15:11.120
The trade winds push this moisture inland, westward over the vast expanse of the Amazon Basin, heading toward the towering wall of the Andes Mountains.

15:11.980 --> 15:16.640
As that air mass travels across the continent, it encounters convection and begins to drop rain.

15:17.440 --> 15:19.860
This process is known as Rayleigh distillation.

15:20.520 --> 15:21.600
Rayleigh distillation.

15:22.180 --> 15:26.440
Because the oxygen-18 water molecules are heavier and condense more readily,

15:26.820 --> 15:31.280
they preferentially fall out of the cloud in the very first rainstorms near the coast.

15:31.280 --> 15:34.300
Ah, the heavy water drops out early.

15:34.860 --> 15:38.040
As the cloud continues its journey thousands of kilometers further inland,

15:38.320 --> 15:43.620
the remaining water vapor in that cloud becomes progressively depleted of the heavy oxygen-18.

15:44.360 --> 15:48.340
The isotopic signature becomes lighter and lighter the further inland you go.

15:48.920 --> 15:50.780
And the more it rains along the way.

15:51.380 --> 15:53.920
So if the South American monsoon is incredibly strong,

15:53.920 --> 15:59.520
like if there is intense, continuous rainfall all across the Amazon Basin upstream of these caves,

15:59.760 --> 16:04.380
by the time that heavily depleted cloud finally reaches the eastern flank of the Andes,

16:04.580 --> 16:09.620
the rain it drops over the cave system is going to have a very low concentration of oxygen-18.

16:10.180 --> 16:11.120
That's it exactly.

16:11.880 --> 16:18.480
Lower delta-18-0 in the cave drip water directly correlates to a stronger, more intense upstream monsoon.

16:18.480 --> 16:23.040
That isotopic signature gets locked into the calcite layer of the stalagmite.

16:23.680 --> 16:27.700
By reading the layers, we're essentially reading the integrated history of rainfall

16:27.700 --> 16:32.160
across the entire Amazon Basin for that specific 50-year block of time.

16:32.600 --> 16:32.860
Okay.

16:33.100 --> 16:36.520
If I'm listening to this and I know how chaotic local weather is,

16:36.580 --> 16:37.580
I'm finding a flaw in the plan.

16:38.020 --> 16:38.180
Oh.

16:38.520 --> 16:40.140
These caves are in the mountains.

16:40.900 --> 16:44.480
Mountains generate their own crazy, unpredictable microclimates.

16:44.480 --> 16:50.560
How can Olson's team be absolutely sure that the stalagmite is recording this giant regional Amazon monsoon

16:50.560 --> 16:55.600
and not just a localized thunderstorm system that hovered over the cave entrance for a decade?

16:56.100 --> 17:00.380
That is the central defining problem of interpreting any paleoclimate proxy.

17:01.440 --> 17:05.040
A stalagmite is not a pristine, isolated scientific instrument.

17:05.280 --> 17:05.500
Right.

17:05.620 --> 17:07.320
It's a rock in a damp hole.

17:07.320 --> 17:11.940
It is responding to multiple overlapping influences simultaneously.

17:12.280 --> 17:15.680
The source water in the Atlantic, the upstream rain out across the basin,

17:16.180 --> 17:18.340
local orographic precipitation hitting the mountain,

17:18.700 --> 17:21.380
and even the ambient temperature inside the cave itself.

17:21.640 --> 17:26.540
So how did they mathematically filter out the local mountain noise to get to the pure regional signal?

17:27.300 --> 17:30.220
They utilized an independent environmental archive.

17:30.920 --> 17:33.480
Roughly 30 kilometers away from the cave sites,

17:33.480 --> 17:38.380
there is a sediment-based proxy record of ancient glacier extent from Lake Hunin.

17:38.840 --> 17:39.100
Okay.

17:39.400 --> 17:43.420
This glacial index responds primarily to local temperature and local precipitation,

17:44.100 --> 17:46.900
specific to that high-altitude Andean environment.

17:47.700 --> 17:50.760
Olson et al. standardized both the cave record and the lake record

17:50.760 --> 17:56.120
and essentially subtracted the Lake Hunin Glacial Index from the Andean cave composite.

17:56.120 --> 18:04.880
The logic being, if you subtract the local mountain weather signal from the combined local regional cave signal,

18:05.080 --> 18:09.440
you are left with an index of pure upstream rain out in the Amazon lowlands.

18:09.600 --> 18:11.640
That is the conceptual framework, yes.

18:12.220 --> 18:13.840
I have to push back on this algebraically.

18:14.300 --> 18:20.660
Subtracting one proxy from another entirely different type of proxy to get a mathematically pure regional rain signal.

18:20.660 --> 18:25.080
If I subtract my local neighborhood's weather data from my state's weather data,

18:25.280 --> 18:28.540
I don't magically get a perfect map of a global weather pattern.

18:28.920 --> 18:32.600
There's so much shared variance, isn't this incredibly risky math?

18:32.960 --> 18:34.960
Your skepticism is entirely warranted,

18:35.520 --> 18:39.300
and it is a point that Flyxion explicitly unpacks in the essay.

18:39.880 --> 18:42.700
The algebraic limits of this kind of subtraction are profound.

18:43.340 --> 18:44.340
Let's look at the variables.

18:45.060 --> 18:46.960
Let's say your cave record is variable X,

18:46.960 --> 18:50.940
and its variance is driven by a combination of local weather and regional weather.

18:51.580 --> 18:54.200
And your lake record is variable G,

18:54.620 --> 18:57.220
which is also driven by local weather and regional weather,

18:57.600 --> 19:00.440
just interacting in different proportions and sensitivities.

19:00.560 --> 19:02.460
Right, they're both responding to the same sky.

19:03.420 --> 19:06.780
Standardizing them to have a mean of zero and a variance of one,

19:07.560 --> 19:11.160
and that subtracting G from X does not mathematically guarantee

19:11.160 --> 19:14.300
that the local variables perfectly cancel each other out to zero.

19:14.300 --> 19:17.340
Furthermore, it certainly does not guarantee

19:17.340 --> 19:20.540
that the regional variables are perfectly preserved in the remainder.

19:20.960 --> 19:21.680
You could lose data.

19:22.140 --> 19:22.380
Yes.

19:23.020 --> 19:27.300
You might accidentally subtract out some of the regional signal you were trying to isolate,

19:27.680 --> 19:30.840
simply because the local glacier also responded to it.

19:31.260 --> 19:37.460
So this subtraction method is not a mathematical magic wand that reveals the pure truth.

19:37.680 --> 19:38.340
It is not.

19:38.340 --> 19:44.600
Flyxion is very careful to emphasize that the validity of this correction relies on external corroboration.

19:45.160 --> 19:49.140
You have to match the resulting subtracted curve with independent records,

19:49.520 --> 19:54.940
like speleothems from the Western Amazon that aren't subject to the same local Andean mountain weather.

