and look down into the oceans.

31:58.880 --> 32:03.040
This brings us to the most interconnected, systemic part of the analysis,

32:03.620 --> 32:07.220
the ocean's long reach, teleconnections and conditional coupling.

32:07.720 --> 32:12.060
Let's start with the Atlantic Ocean because it seems to be the prime suspect in these terminations.

32:12.420 --> 32:18.980
Specifically, we need to examine the Atlantic Meridional Overturning Circulation, or the AMOC.

32:19.100 --> 32:19.880
Okay, the AMOC.

32:19.880 --> 32:25.580
This is a colossal system of deep ocean currents that acts as a global conveyor belt.

32:26.120 --> 32:32.460
It carries massive amounts of warm tropical surface water northward across the equator deep into the North Atlantic.

32:32.760 --> 32:32.860
Right.

32:33.200 --> 32:40.040
As it reaches the north, the water cools, becomes denser, sinks to the abyssal plain, and flows back southward.

32:40.040 --> 32:44.280
This conveyor belt fundamentally keeps the northern hemisphere much warmer

32:44.280 --> 32:47.460
than it would otherwise be based on solar radiation alone.

32:48.120 --> 32:52.580
But during a glacial termination, when the ice age is violently ending and the globe is warming,

32:53.060 --> 32:58.380
you have massive, continent-spanning ice sheets covering North America and Europe that begin to disintegrate.

32:58.380 --> 33:04.820
As they melt, they dump astronomical volumes of freezing freshwater directly into the North Atlantic Ocean.

33:05.300 --> 33:09.520
And freshwater, lacking the dissolved salt of seawater, is significantly less dense.

33:10.140 --> 33:11.180
It refuses to sink.

33:11.700 --> 33:13.080
It just floats on top.

33:13.360 --> 33:19.400
It pools on the surface, creating a massive, buoyant lid that physically chokes off the sinking mechanism

33:19.400 --> 33:22.440
that drives the entire AMOC conveyor belt.

33:22.440 --> 33:28.180
The overturning circulation drastically weakens, or even collapses entirely.

33:28.920 --> 33:29.040
Wow.

33:29.640 --> 33:35.200
The oceanic transport of heat grinds to a halt, and the North Atlantic region cools dramatically,

33:35.800 --> 33:41.460
ironically plunging into a deep freeze, even while the rest of the globe is experiencing rapid warming.

33:42.240 --> 33:47.340
So older climate theories, like the influential one published by Chang et al. back in 2016,

33:48.040 --> 33:51.040
suggested a fairly straightforward reaction to this.

33:51.040 --> 33:56.200
They argued that if the North Atlantic gets incredibly cold, the intertropical convergence zone,

33:56.360 --> 33:59.520
the rain belt, would simply flee southward to find warmth.

33:59.960 --> 34:05.060
Heat pools in the equatorial South Atlantic, the North gets cold and dry, the South gets warm and wet.

34:05.360 --> 34:07.580
The seesaw just tilts heavily to the South.

34:08.180 --> 34:12.660
But Olsen's pristine stalagmite data directly contradicts that simple southern shift theory.

34:13.380 --> 34:15.420
The South American monsoon weakened too.

34:15.900 --> 34:17.000
The seesaw didn't tilt.

34:17.780 --> 34:18.360
It broke.

34:19.400 --> 34:20.740
It's never that simple.

34:21.040 --> 34:25.920
And this is where the oceanic physics become incredibly complex and unintuitive.

34:26.620 --> 34:29.780
To understand how a cold Atlantic dries out South America,

34:30.740 --> 34:34.060
Olsen et al. looked at multi-model hosing experiments,

34:34.480 --> 34:39.460
specifically drawing from the North Atlantic Hosing Model Intercomparison Project, or NOMIP.

34:39.460 --> 34:44.540
Let's explain what a hosing experiment actually is in the context of climate modeling.

34:44.540 --> 34:49.540
In computational climate dynamics, a hosing experiment is a stress test.

34:50.480 --> 34:57.920
Modelers take a highly complex simulation of the Earth's climate and mathematically force a massive, unnatural flux of fresh water,

34:58.020 --> 35:02.940
like turning on a giant fire hose, directly into the simulated North Atlantic.

35:02.940 --> 35:04.600
They intentionally break it.

35:04.600 --> 35:10.280
They artificially collapse the AMOC just to see what breaks downstream in the global weather systems.

35:11.020 --> 35:15.200
And what did the NAHMIP simulations reveal when they hosed the Atlantic?

35:15.200 --> 35:23.180
They found that weakening the AMOC does not simply push the ITC-Z rainbelts southward in a clean, linear fashion.

35:23.900 --> 35:28.980
Instead, the extreme cooling in the North triggers a massive reorganization of atmospheric pressure.

35:29.340 --> 35:30.060
Okay.

35:30.060 --> 35:36.260
It causes a complex, highly heterogeneous drying pattern across both Asia and the Andes

35:36.260 --> 35:40.500
by fundamentally strengthening and expanding the subtropical high-pressure systems.

35:40.880 --> 35:44.840
It's like throwing a heavy steel wrench into a finely tuned gearbox.

35:45.100 --> 35:50.760
It doesn't just make the gears cleanly turn in reverse, it makes the entire engine block shudder, warp, and strip its teeth.

35:51.340 --> 35:54.360
The disruption radiates outward in totally unpredictable ways.

35:55.100 --> 35:57.460
But wait, that's just the Atlantic mechanism.

35:57.460 --> 36:01.080
Flyxion also deeply analyzes the Pacific connection, doesn't he?

