36:38.140 --> 36:42.600
the chilled air suppresses thermal convection over the tropical Atlantic and Central America.

36:43.080 --> 36:44.700
Okay, the air stops rising.

36:44.700 --> 36:51.200
This suppression alters the trade winds and triggers a chain reaction that physically shifts the sinking

36:51.200 --> 36:53.900
and rising branches of the Walker cells in the Pacific.

36:54.380 --> 36:57.760
I want to make sure I'm following the physical chain of events here.

36:57.940 --> 37:02.880
Are we literally saying that a localized patch of cold water in the North Atlantic Ocean

37:02.880 --> 37:05.540
reached completely across the globe,

37:06.320 --> 37:08.860
messed with the atmospheric flow over the Pacific Ocean,

37:08.860 --> 37:13.500
and ultimately turned off the rain falling on the Amazon rainforest?

37:14.140 --> 37:16.500
We are tracing exactly that causal chain.

37:17.000 --> 37:19.240
As the atmospheric circulation shifts,

37:19.600 --> 37:22.260
the eastern Pacific Ocean, near the coast of South America,

37:22.640 --> 37:26.180
begins to warm anomalously in response to the changing winds.

37:26.840 --> 37:28.060
The water gets warmer.

37:28.060 --> 37:34.100
Yes, and this shift in the locus of tropical heating generates massive atmospheric ripples

37:34.100 --> 37:36.460
known as tropical Rossby waves.

37:36.980 --> 37:41.840
Rossby waves are those massive planetary scale meanders in high-altitude winds, right?

37:41.960 --> 37:43.640
Like the jet stream looping wildly.

37:44.060 --> 37:48.680
Yes, they are planetary waves driven by the conservation of potential vorticity.

37:49.380 --> 37:51.800
When this abnormal heating occurs in the eastern Pacific,

37:51.800 --> 37:59.300
it forces a stationary Rossby wave response that manifests as twin, powerful high-pressure anticyclones

37:59.300 --> 38:00.640
straddling the equator.

38:01.200 --> 38:02.380
Twin anticyclones.

38:02.920 --> 38:07.520
And crucially, these high-pressure domes park themselves directly over the northern Amazon basin.

38:08.120 --> 38:09.900
And high-pressure means sinking air?

38:10.080 --> 38:12.620
If the air is being pushed down by these anticyclones,

38:12.940 --> 38:19.100
it physically cannot rise to form the towering deep convective clouds required for a monsoon downpour.

38:19.560 --> 38:20.040
Exactly.

38:20.040 --> 38:25.860
The moisture from the Atlantic is still blowing in, but the atmosphere acts like a physical lid.

38:26.280 --> 38:28.300
The convection is mathematically suppressed.

38:28.640 --> 38:33.340
So a melting glacier in Canada collapses a current in the Atlantic,

38:33.780 --> 38:35.600
which warms the eastern Pacific,

38:36.420 --> 38:39.120
which creates an invisible dome of high pressure over Brazil,

38:39.300 --> 38:41.900
which stops the rain from falling on a cave in Peru.

38:42.100 --> 38:45.020
That level of interconnectedness is terrifying.

38:45.020 --> 38:50.780
It perfectly illustrates the formal dynamical concept of state-dependent response, or conditional coupling.

38:50.960 --> 38:52.640
State-dependent response.

38:52.640 --> 38:58.340
The core idea here is that the massive components of the Earth system—the volume of the ice sheets,

38:58.580 --> 39:03.940
the concentration of greenhouse gases, the state of the ocean currents—they aren't just passive background scenery.

39:04.500 --> 39:07.200
They are highly active, dynamical variables.

39:07.200 --> 39:16.720
They set the boundary conditions of the entire planet, and those boundary conditions explicitly dictate how the regional climate reacts to the steady orbital wobble.

39:16.720 --> 39:22.280
So if the planetary boundary conditions change, like swapping out a warm Atlantic for a cold one,

39:22.800 --> 39:29.040
the exact same 23,000-year celestial wobble produces a totally unrecognizable pattern of rainfall.

39:29.600 --> 39:30.060
Precisely.

39:30.060 --> 39:36.880
When the inter-hemispheric insulation contrast—the orbital seesaw—is the dominant thermodynamic force,

39:37.140 --> 39:39.900
you get the beautiful alternating monsoon pattern.

39:40.120 --> 39:40.760
The metronome.

39:40.940 --> 39:45.000
But when a collapsing ice sheet causes a massive, overriding oceanic shock,

39:45.420 --> 39:49.460
that new, violent influence drowns out the gentle orbital wobble.

39:50.320 --> 39:51.980
The celestial forcing is still happening.

39:52.800 --> 39:53.900
The math hasn't changed.

39:54.180 --> 39:58.840
But its terrestrial consequences are completely masked by the traumatized state of the oceans.

39:58.840 --> 40:03.700
This realization brings us to one of the most intellectually satisfying parts of Flyxion's essay.

40:04.480 --> 40:07.140
Flyxion doesn't just describe this interplay conceptually.

40:07.460 --> 40:14.100
They introduce a rigorous mathematical framework to prove how the orbital seesaw and these violent oceanic shocks

40:14.100 --> 40:18.260
can actually coexist in the data without mathematically canceling each other out.

40:18.780 --> 40:22.080
It is a brilliant, clarifying mathematical decomposition.

40:22.920 --> 40:26.040
Flyxion proposes that to truly understand the proxy record,

40:26.040 --> 40:35.000
we have to separate the monsoon variants into two distinct mathematical modes—the difference mode and the common mode.

40:35.000 --> 40:40.820
Okay, let's carefully walk through the algebra of this for the listener because it's the key that unlocks the whole mystery.

40:41.580 --> 40:42.620
It's straightforward algebra.

40:43.360 --> 40:49.540
Let's designate the rainfall anomaly in the northern hemisphere, the Asian monsoon, as variable Xn.

40:49.960 --> 40:56.920
And let's designate the rainfall anomaly in the southern hemisphere, the South American monsoon, as variable Xs.

40:56.920 --> 41:00.960
Okay, we have our two monsoons, Xn and Xs.

41:01.600 --> 41:05.800
Flyxion defines the difference mode, which we will call D, with a simple equation.

41:06.580 --> 41:11.080
D equals the quantity of Xn minus Xs divided by 2.

41:11.500 --> 41:11.920
Got it.

41:12.400 --> 41:15.140
This mathematical mode represents the orbital seesaw.

41:15.520 --> 41:21.300
It perfectly isolates and measures the opposition, the pure contrast, between the two hemispheres.

41:21.300 --> 41:25.740
Right. It measures how far apart their weather patterns are at any given moment.

41:25.880 --> 41:26.280
Exactly.

41:27.040 --> 41:30.800
Then Flyxion defines the common mode, which we will call C.

41:31.400 --> 41:38.040
The equation for the common mode is C equals the quantity of Xn plus Xs divided by 2.

41:38.440 --> 41:40.160
Okay, addition instead of subtraction.

41:40.480 --> 41:43.000
This mode isolates the simultaneous variance.

41:43.500 --> 41:48.600
It represents the strength of an external shock that is hitting both hemispheres at the exact same time,

41:48.960 --> 41:50.240
pushing them in the same direction.

41:50.240 --> 41:55.160
And the absolute revelation of structuring the data this way is that mathematically,

41:55.600 --> 42:01.200
a massive catastrophic plunge in the common mode can make both monsoons appear to dry up entirely,

42:01.520 --> 42:05.860
even while the difference mode continues to oscillate perfectly in the background.

42:06.360 --> 42:10.300
Let's apply some hypothetical numbers to the equations to make the physics concrete.

42:11.120 --> 42:16.300
Imagine a scenario where the common mode, C, drops to a severe value of negative 2.

42:16.640 --> 42:16.880
Okay.

42:16.880 --> 42:21.040
This represents a massive oceanic shock, like an AMOC collapse,

42:21.440 --> 42:25.000
that is applying a strong drying force to both hemispheres simultaneously.

42:25.800 --> 42:28.500
But at that exact same moment in geological time,

42:28.900 --> 42:33.600
let's say the difference mode, D, is sitting at a value of plus 0.5,

42:34.040 --> 42:38.440
because the orbital seesaw happens to be tilted slightly, favoring rain in the north.

42:38.440 --> 42:40.000
Okay, I'll do the algebra.

42:40.000 --> 42:45.060
Based on Flyxion's decomposition, the actual observed northern rainfall, Xn,

42:45.220 --> 42:48.140
is simply equal to the common mode plus the difference mode.

42:48.300 --> 42:49.340
So, C plus D.

42:49.840 --> 42:54.400
That's negative 2 plus 0.5, which equals negative 1.5.

42:54.840 --> 42:57.160
The Asian monsoon registers as very dry.

42:57.980 --> 42:58.480
Correct.

42:58.480 --> 43:05.280
And the southern rainfall, Xs, is equal to the common mode minus the difference mode, C minus D.

43:06.060 --> 43:10.400
That's negative 2 minus 0.5, which equals negative 2.5.

43:10.840 --> 43:14.300
The South American monsoon registers as extremely dry.

43:14.760 --> 43:15.780
Look at the result.

43:15.780 --> 43:20.680
Both monsoons are registering as anomalously dry at the exact same time.

43:21.420 --> 43:24.260
The synchronous failure is fully triggered.

43:24.860 --> 43:28.480
But if you look at the underlying math, the difference between them,

43:28.740 --> 43:31.600
the gap between minus 1.5 and minus 2.5,

43:31.880 --> 43:34.980
is still entirely driven by the orbital seesaw.

43:35.500 --> 43:38.120
It's still exactly a difference of 1.

43:38.660 --> 43:40.780
The dipole never actually died.

43:41.420 --> 43:44.340
The physics of the orbital wobble were still operating perfectly,

43:44.340 --> 43:46.780
shifting the relative balance of moisture.

43:47.180 --> 43:48.120
They were just overwhelmed,

43:48.440 --> 43:52.080
drowned out by a massive negative baseline shift in the common mode.

43:52.800 --> 43:55.480
I love thinking about this in terms of audio engineering.

43:55.820 --> 43:58.700
It's exactly like listening to a stereo system

43:58.700 --> 44:02.800
where the left speaker and the right speaker are taking turns playing a melody.

44:03.040 --> 44:04.960
Left, right, left, right.

44:05.340 --> 44:06.380
Panning back and forth.

44:06.780 --> 44:07.480
Like the seesaw.

44:07.760 --> 44:07.960
Yeah.

44:08.440 --> 44:10.380
That panning melody is your dipole.

44:10.640 --> 44:11.560
That's your difference mode.

44:11.560 --> 44:15.720
But suddenly, a producer reaches over to the mixing board

44:15.720 --> 44:19.600
and grabs the master volume fader and pulls it way, way down.

44:20.180 --> 44:22.460
That master volume fader is the common mode.

44:22.700 --> 44:25.460
That is a phenomenal conceptual map for the mathematics.

44:25.760 --> 44:28.520
The left and right speakers are technically still taking turns.

44:29.080 --> 44:33.500
The internal electronic wiring is still routing the electrical signal left and right perfectly.

44:33.500 --> 44:35.080
The seesaw is intact.

44:35.820 --> 44:38.160
But because the master volume has been crushed,

44:38.500 --> 44:43.160
the acoustic output in the room is so low that you basically can't hear the music from either speaker.

44:43.500 --> 44:45.880
To an untrained observer standing in the room,

44:46.020 --> 44:48.800
it just sounds like both speakers broke at the same time.

44:48.920 --> 44:49.860
But they aren't broken.

44:50.360 --> 44:51.120
They are just quiet.

44:51.120 --> 44:56.100
I heavily endorse that analogy because it highlights the exact analytical trap

44:56.100 --> 44:57.860
Flyxion is warning us about.

44:58.460 --> 45:02.940
A simplified observational model assumes that if you don't hear the melody clearly,

45:03.240 --> 45:06.340
the internal wiring of the stereo must be fundamentally destroyed.

45:06.740 --> 45:10.280
But the wiring, the difference mode, is perfectly fine.

45:11.240 --> 45:14.300
Now, what adds an extra layer of complexity to reality

45:14.300 --> 45:18.860
is that these modes aren't perfectly hermetically isolated from each other.

45:19.220 --> 45:19.820
What do you mean?

45:19.820 --> 45:22.720
Earth is not a perfectly symmetrical sphere.

45:23.420 --> 45:26.680
There are massive geographical asymmetries between the hemispheres.

45:27.200 --> 45:31.120
The northern hemisphere contains vastly more continental land mass,

45:31.360 --> 45:32.700
Eurasia and North America,

45:33.120 --> 45:35.580
while the southern hemisphere is dominated by ocean.

45:35.880 --> 45:35.980
Right.

45:36.460 --> 45:38.960
The distribution of mountain ranges is different.

45:39.080 --> 45:41.420
The geography of the ice sheets is different.

45:42.140 --> 45:46.220
These physical asymmetries generate complex mathematical coupling

45:46.220 --> 45:48.440
between the common and difference modes.

45:48.440 --> 45:50.640
So, extending the stereo analogy,

45:51.160 --> 45:53.820
the master volume knob might occasionally glitch out

45:53.820 --> 45:57.500
and suppress the left speaker slightly more than it suppresses the right speaker,

45:57.600 --> 45:59.560
just because they are built slightly differently.

45:59.940 --> 46:00.460
Precisely.

46:01.140 --> 46:05.040
The hemispheric asymmetries ensure that a globally applied common mode shock

46:05.040 --> 46:08.680
does not manifest identically on both sides of the equator,

46:08.680 --> 46:12.340
which perfectly explains the quirks in the cave data.

46:12.340 --> 46:13.840
Ah, the weird anomalies.

46:14.660 --> 46:16.620
It explains why during the glacial terminations,

46:17.320 --> 46:20.820
the Asian monsoon acted weirder and decoupled more violently,

46:21.560 --> 46:23.580
whereas during the interglacial periods,

46:23.720 --> 46:25.860
the South American monsoon was the one that rebelled.

46:26.700 --> 46:29.860
The local geography dictates how the global shock is absorbed.

46:29.860 --> 46:32.040
Which brings us to the most dramatic,

46:32.240 --> 46:34.200
high-stakes part of this entire deep dive,

46:34.580 --> 46:35.600
the tipping point debate.

46:36.200 --> 46:37.160
A contentious topic.

46:37.560 --> 46:38.260
It really is.

46:38.780 --> 46:42.020
Did the climate actually break permanently during these events?

46:42.520 --> 46:44.560
We know the common mode was violently disrupted.

46:45.120 --> 46:47.600
We know the master volume was turned down for centuries,

46:47.740 --> 46:48.640
sometimes millennia.

46:49.080 --> 46:51.740
But was this just a temporary shock to the system,

46:51.920 --> 46:55.640
or did the Earth's climate cross a terrifying point of no return?

46:56.320 --> 46:59.840
Flyxion handles this question with an incredible degree of mathematical rigor,

47:00.580 --> 47:03.880
separating three distinct, mutually exclusive explanations

47:03.880 --> 47:05.380
for these synchronous anomalies.

47:05.980 --> 47:07.640
In popular climate communication,

47:08.080 --> 47:11.300
it is incredibly easy, and frankly incentivized,

47:11.480 --> 47:12.360
to just yell,

47:12.660 --> 47:15.600
tipping point, whenever a graph goes sharply down.

47:16.000 --> 47:18.400
But Flyxion demands strict dynamical precision.

47:18.500 --> 47:21.480
Let's walk through all three of Flyxion's explanations.

47:21.720 --> 47:23.700
What is explanation number one?

47:23.700 --> 47:26.580
Number one is defined as a forced excursion.

47:27.340 --> 47:31.220
This means a temporary external push was applied to a stable system.

47:31.740 --> 47:35.820
Imagine a melting glacier dumping fresh water into the Atlantic.

47:36.920 --> 47:39.940
The system is pushed far away from its normal equilibrium state,

47:40.140 --> 47:43.400
but the underlying mathematical landscape of the climate dynamics

47:43.400 --> 47:45.040
never actually changes.

47:45.420 --> 47:46.480
It just bends.

47:47.040 --> 47:48.560
Once the glacier finishes melting,

47:48.940 --> 47:50.440
and the fresh water stops flowing,

47:50.440 --> 47:54.120
the system simply relaxes back to its original normal state.

47:54.640 --> 47:57.580
The stereo volume knob was turned down by a heavy hand,

47:58.020 --> 47:59.060
and the moment the hand let go,

47:59.540 --> 48:01.080
the fader sprang back up to the top.

48:01.500 --> 48:04.020
Okay, a forced excursion feels safe.

48:04.680 --> 48:06.060
It's a severe weather event,

48:06.180 --> 48:07.700
but it's fundamentally reversible.

48:08.220 --> 48:09.380
The climate heals itself.

48:09.940 --> 48:11.300
What is explanation number two?

48:11.680 --> 48:13.960
Number two is state-dependent coupling.

48:14.480 --> 48:17.160
This implies that the system didn't just receive a push.

48:17.160 --> 48:20.240
The system itself was temporarily highly sensitive

48:20.240 --> 48:22.220
because of its specific boundary conditions.

48:22.980 --> 48:24.600
The relaxation rate of the climate,

48:25.000 --> 48:27.100
how fast it can bounce back from a shock,

48:27.540 --> 48:30.000
was radically altered because of the background state

48:30.000 --> 48:33.000
of the melting ice and the rapidly rising CO2.

48:33.320 --> 48:35.180
So the system itself was fragile.

48:35.440 --> 48:37.660
The exact same freshwater perturbation,

48:38.100 --> 48:40.720
if applied 10,000 years earlier or later,

48:41.120 --> 48:43.060
wouldn't have caused the monsoons to fail at all.

48:43.060 --> 48:46.100
But because the Earth was in a precarious,

48:46.200 --> 48:47.940
highly sensitive transitional state,

48:48.340 --> 48:49.860
it was susceptible to the shock.

48:50.680 --> 48:53.000
So it's not just a heavy hand on the volume knob.

48:53.100 --> 48:55.720
It's that the internal mechanisms of the knob itself

48:55.720 --> 48:57.280
were temporarily altered,

48:57.700 --> 48:59.660
making it hypersensitive to even a light touch.

49:00.300 --> 49:02.220
But again, under this explanation,

49:02.460 --> 49:04.700
once the boundary conditions finally stabilize

49:04.700 --> 49:06.060
and the transition ends,

49:06.520 --> 49:07.780
the system goes back to normal.

49:07.880 --> 49:08.760
It's still reversible.

49:09.300 --> 49:09.400
Right.

49:09.400 --> 49:11.820
But then we have the third explanation,

49:12.240 --> 49:13.300
which is the one that generates

49:13.300 --> 49:14.540
all the terrifying headlines.

49:15.400 --> 49:18.200
Explanation number three is a true regime transition,

49:18.760 --> 49:20.800
defined mathematically as a bifurcation.

49:21.220 --> 49:22.000
The tipping point.

49:22.220 --> 49:22.440
Yes.

49:23.360 --> 49:24.340
In this strict reading,

49:24.620 --> 49:26.960
the climate crossed a critical stability threshold.

49:27.440 --> 49:29.980
It completely lost its original equilibrium state.

49:30.500 --> 49:32.320
The mathematics governing it broke down,

49:32.660 --> 49:34.300
and it snapped into a brand new,

49:34.420 --> 49:36.240
entirely different stable state.

49:36.240 --> 49:40.460
Flyxion uses something called a cubic model of bifurcation

49:40.460 --> 49:42.080
in the essay to explain this,

49:42.200 --> 49:42.680
and honestly,

49:42.780 --> 49:45.480
it gets pretty deep into the weeds of topological mathematics.

49:46.080 --> 49:48.240
They talk about pitchfork bifurcations

49:48.240 --> 49:50.400
and saddle node bifurcations.

49:51.280 --> 49:54.120
Can you translate what a mathematical bifurcation

49:54.120 --> 49:56.500
actually looks and feels like for the listener

49:56.500 --> 49:58.960
without using differential equations?

49:59.600 --> 50:01.880
Let's use a topological landscape analogy.

50:02.280 --> 50:03.200
Think of a heavy marble

50:03.200 --> 50:05.800
resting at the bottom of a smooth, concave bowl.

50:06.720 --> 50:08.080
The bottom of the bowl represents

50:08.080 --> 50:10.220
a stable equilibrium state for the climate.

50:10.940 --> 50:11.980
If you flick the marble,

50:12.260 --> 50:13.620
which is a forced excursion,

50:13.940 --> 50:15.500
it rolls up the side of the bowl,

50:15.860 --> 50:17.920
but gravity inevitably pulls it back down

50:17.920 --> 50:19.400
until it settles in the bottom again.

50:19.720 --> 50:19.980
Okay.

50:20.220 --> 50:21.420
The marble always finds home.

50:22.180 --> 50:24.140
Now imagine the bowl is made of flexible rubber,

50:24.700 --> 50:27.220
and you start slowly, relentlessly

50:27.220 --> 50:28.860
pushing up from the bottom center

50:28.860 --> 50:30.740
right underneath the marble.

50:31.140 --> 50:32.900
The bowl starts to flatten out.

50:32.900 --> 50:33.720
Oh, I see.

50:33.720 --> 50:36.620
The marble is still technically sitting in the middle,

50:36.880 --> 50:38.140
but the slope is gone.

50:38.860 --> 50:40.000
Now, if you flick it,

50:40.060 --> 50:41.240
it rolls much further

50:41.240 --> 50:43.540
and takes much longer to settle back.

50:43.680 --> 50:44.640
It's precarious.

50:45.260 --> 50:46.540
That flattening landscape

50:46.540 --> 50:48.960
is state-dependent susceptibility.

50:48.960 --> 50:50.660
And a bifurcation,

50:51.000 --> 50:51.920
a true tipping point.

50:52.380 --> 50:53.600
You keep pushing up from the bottom

50:53.600 --> 50:54.560
until the center of the bowl

50:54.560 --> 50:56.160
suddenly pops upward,

50:56.760 --> 50:58.020
completely inverting the center.

50:58.600 --> 50:59.680
What used to be a valley

50:59.680 --> 51:00.700
is now a hill.

51:01.520 --> 51:01.840
Suddenly,

51:02.120 --> 51:03.480
resting in the exact center

51:03.480 --> 51:04.900
is physically impossible.

51:05.260 --> 51:06.300
Because it's a peak.

51:06.500 --> 51:08.380
The marble must roll off the hill,

51:08.620 --> 51:10.240
either to the left or to the right,

51:10.600 --> 51:12.100
into a completely new groove

51:12.100 --> 51:12.760
that is formed.

51:13.260 --> 51:14.800
The original stable state,

51:14.800 --> 51:15.700
the center valley,

51:16.060 --> 51:18.020
has been mathematically destroyed

51:18.020 --> 51:19.460
and replaced by two new

51:19.460 --> 51:20.920
alternative stable states.

51:21.600 --> 51:23.340
That specific topological change

51:23.340 --> 51:25.260
is known as a pitchfork bifurcation.

51:26.000 --> 51:28.220
And if it was a saddle node bifurcation?

51:28.620 --> 51:30.300
In a saddle node bifurcation,

51:30.440 --> 51:31.560
you don't push up the center.

51:32.120 --> 51:33.760
You tilt the entire bowl

51:33.760 --> 51:35.000
until the lip of the bowl

51:35.000 --> 51:36.060
merges with the bottom.

51:36.700 --> 51:37.440
And the marble suddenly

51:37.440 --> 51:38.580
just drops off a cliff

51:38.580 --> 51:39.280
into free fall

51:39.280 --> 51:40.540
until it hits a lower surface.

51:40.540 --> 51:42.820
The entire original attractor

51:42.820 --> 51:44.100
vanishes in an instant.

51:44.940 --> 51:46.440
Okay, so if we assume

51:46.440 --> 51:48.740
a pitchfork bifurcation happened

51:48.740 --> 51:50.620
and the marble is now stuck

51:50.620 --> 51:51.520
in a new groove

51:51.520 --> 51:52.700
on the left side of the bowl,

51:52.940 --> 51:55.340
if you stop pushing on the rubber,

51:55.680 --> 51:57.200
if you remove the shock,

51:57.620 --> 51:58.860
does the marble just

51:58.860 --> 51:59.960
roll back to the center?

52:00.500 --> 52:01.240
Not necessarily.

52:02.120 --> 52:03.600
And this is the most terrifying part

52:03.600 --> 52:04.200
of Tipping Points.

52:04.620 --> 52:05.840
Once the marble is locked

52:05.840 --> 52:06.600
in the new groove,

52:06.700 --> 52:07.580
it is trapped there

52:07.580 --> 52:09.300
by a new local gravity well.

52:09.300 --> 52:11.720
Even if you slowly relax

52:11.720 --> 52:12.340
the rubber bowl

52:12.340 --> 52:13.480
back to its original shape,

52:13.780 --> 52:15.020
the marble might stay stuck

52:15.020 --> 52:15.800
off to the side

52:15.800 --> 52:17.640
for a very long time

52:17.640 --> 52:18.860
until the bowl is nearly

52:18.860 --> 52:19.900
perfectly flat again

52:19.900 --> 52:21.420
and it finally slips back.

52:22.160 --> 52:22.660
It gets stuck.

52:23.160 --> 52:24.120
That delay,

52:24.720 --> 52:26.140
that failure of a system

52:26.140 --> 52:27.500
to return to its original state

52:27.500 --> 52:28.620
along the same path

52:28.620 --> 52:29.520
it took to leave it,

52:29.880 --> 52:31.240
is a mathematical property

52:31.240 --> 52:32.520
called hysteresis.

52:33.140 --> 52:34.680
So a true tipping point,

52:34.840 --> 52:35.660
a bifurcation,

52:36.320 --> 52:37.920
means the climate gets stuck

52:37.920 --> 52:39.120
in a new reality.

52:39.640 --> 52:40.400
Simply removing

52:40.400 --> 52:41.360
the carbon emissions

52:41.360 --> 52:43.040
or stopping the freshwater shock

52:43.040 --> 52:45.100
doesn't magically fix the system.

52:45.540 --> 52:46.900
It requires massive,

52:47.280 --> 52:48.300
disproportionate force

52:48.300 --> 52:49.080
to push the marble

52:49.080 --> 52:49.780
back up the hill.

52:50.160 --> 52:50.520
Exactly.

52:51.080 --> 52:52.340
And now to evaluate the data.

52:53.420 --> 52:55.260
The Olsen et al. source paper

52:55.260 --> 52:56.660
repeatedly uses the word

52:56.660 --> 52:57.940
reorganization

52:57.940 --> 52:59.360
to describe these

52:59.360 --> 53:00.480
anomalous dry events.

53:00.960 --> 53:01.780
But Flyxion

53:01.780 --> 53:03.060
expertly points out

53:03.060 --> 53:03.640
that the authors

53:03.640 --> 53:04.620
are using that word

53:04.620 --> 53:05.460
descriptively

53:05.460 --> 53:06.340
to talk about

53:06.340 --> 53:07.440
a change in patterns,

53:07.600 --> 53:08.740
not mathematically

53:08.740 --> 53:09.500
to claim

53:09.500 --> 53:10.740
a bifurcation occurred.

53:11.520 --> 53:13.040
So what does this all mean

53:13.040 --> 53:14.060
for the current discourse?

53:14.700 --> 53:16.080
I really feel

53:16.080 --> 53:17.300
the need to push back

53:17.300 --> 53:18.280
on the massive hype

53:18.280 --> 53:18.960
around climate

53:18.960 --> 53:19.800
tipping points here.

53:19.800 --> 53:20.840
Because if you read

53:20.840 --> 53:21.720
the science sections

53:21.720 --> 53:22.400
of the news today,

53:22.860 --> 53:24.140
literally every single

53:24.140 --> 53:25.100
ecological change

53:25.100 --> 53:25.560
is framed

53:25.560 --> 53:26.500
as a tipping point.

53:26.700 --> 53:26.780
True.

53:26.780 --> 53:28.080
The AMOC is tipping.

53:28.520 --> 53:29.460
The Amazon rainforest

53:29.460 --> 53:29.960
is tipping.

53:30.080 --> 53:30.900
The Greenland ice sheets

53:30.900 --> 53:31.260
are tipping.

53:31.940 --> 53:32.500
Everyone,

53:32.820 --> 53:33.460
from journalists

53:33.460 --> 53:34.240
to activists,

53:34.460 --> 53:35.460
loves a dramatic,

53:35.820 --> 53:36.440
catastrophic

53:36.440 --> 53:37.580
tipping point narrative.

53:38.220 --> 53:39.280
But is Flyxion

53:39.280 --> 53:40.260
basically arguing

53:40.260 --> 53:40.840
that we need to

53:40.840 --> 53:41.920
calm down,

53:42.540 --> 53:43.380
look at the strict

53:43.380 --> 53:44.940
calculus of dynamical systems,

53:45.060 --> 53:46.000
and stop crying wolf

53:46.000 --> 53:46.740
about permanent

53:46.740 --> 53:47.500
regime shifts?

53:48.120 --> 53:48.940
This raises

53:48.940 --> 53:49.820
an important question

53:49.820 --> 53:50.780
about the evidential

53:50.780 --> 53:51.560
bar we use.

53:52.260 --> 53:53.240
Flyxion is forcing us

53:53.240 --> 53:53.700
to confront

53:53.700 --> 53:54.940
a highly rigorous standard.

53:55.640 --> 53:56.320
Flyxion argues

53:56.320 --> 53:57.400
that the proxy data

53:57.400 --> 53:58.140
from the caves

53:58.140 --> 53:59.820
definitively proves

53:59.820 --> 54:01.120
that explanation

54:01.120 --> 54:01.840
number one

54:01.840 --> 54:02.440
or number two

54:02.440 --> 54:02.840
happened,

54:03.200 --> 54:04.300
either a severe

54:04.300 --> 54:05.460
forced excursion

54:05.460 --> 54:06.660
or a period

54:06.660 --> 54:07.160
of heightened

54:07.160 --> 54:08.240
state-dependent coupling.

54:08.680 --> 54:09.420
But not number three.

54:09.940 --> 54:10.520
But crucially,

54:11.000 --> 54:12.060
the data does not

54:12.060 --> 54:13.000
contain the necessary

54:13.000 --> 54:14.020
mathematical signatures

54:14.020 --> 54:15.380
to prove

54:15.380 --> 54:16.020
that a true

54:16.020 --> 54:16.600
bifurcation

54:16.600 --> 54:17.600
or tipping point occurred.

54:17.800 --> 54:18.300
Just because

54:18.300 --> 54:19.400
an anomaly is severe

54:19.400 --> 54:20.100
and just because

54:20.100 --> 54:21.240
it lasts a thousand years

54:21.240 --> 54:22.080
doesn't mean

54:22.080 --> 54:22.640
the fundamental

54:22.640 --> 54:23.960
mathematical attractors

54:23.960 --> 54:25.000
of the planetary system

54:25.000 --> 54:25.780
have permanently

54:25.780 --> 54:26.460
mutated.

54:26.860 --> 54:27.240
Precisely.

54:28.020 --> 54:28.840
To a human being,

54:29.020 --> 54:29.920
a thousand-year drought

54:29.920 --> 54:30.700
is an apocalypse.

54:31.300 --> 54:32.460
To a stalagmite

54:32.460 --> 54:33.860
recording a half-million-year

54:33.860 --> 54:34.360
history,

54:34.800 --> 54:35.420
a thousand years

54:35.420 --> 54:36.240
is a minor blip.

54:36.440 --> 54:36.700
Right.

54:37.280 --> 54:38.180
The fact that

54:38.180 --> 54:39.020
the global monsoons

54:39.020 --> 54:39.940
eventually reverted

54:39.940 --> 54:40.860
back to their strict

54:40.860 --> 54:41.840
antiphased

54:41.840 --> 54:42.700
seesaw behavior

54:42.700 --> 54:43.700
after every single

54:43.700 --> 54:44.220
one of these

54:44.220 --> 54:45.260
catastrophic events

54:45.260 --> 54:46.620
strongly implies

54:46.620 --> 54:47.740
that the underlying

54:47.740 --> 54:48.640
stability of the

54:48.640 --> 54:49.260
climate system

54:49.260 --> 54:50.240
wasn't destroyed.

54:50.800 --> 54:51.360
It was merely

54:51.360 --> 54:53.020
obscured by a massive

54:53.020 --> 54:54.120
temporary forcing.

54:54.120 --> 54:55.100
The volume knob

54:55.100 --> 54:56.080
was just turned down.

54:56.960 --> 54:57.760
Flyxion is warning

54:57.760 --> 54:58.800
the scientific community

54:58.800 --> 54:59.520
not to assume

54:59.520 --> 55:00.200
a catastrophic

55:00.200 --> 55:01.260
regime transition

55:01.260 --> 55:02.320
when a highly

55:02.320 --> 55:03.200
sensitive but

55:03.200 --> 55:04.280
ultimately reversible

55:04.280 --> 55:05.780
forced excursion

55:05.780 --> 55:07.000
explains the physical

55:07.000 --> 55:08.140
data perfectly well.

55:08.360 --> 55:09.420
Which is such a

55:09.420 --> 55:10.380
vital grounding warning

55:10.380 --> 55:11.140
for modern climate

55:11.140 --> 55:11.580
science.

55:12.120 --> 55:12.940
But acknowledging

55:12.940 --> 55:13.620
that uncertainty

55:13.620 --> 55:14.780
leads us right into

55:14.780 --> 55:15.860
the final major

55:15.860 --> 55:16.520
problem of this

55:16.520 --> 55:16.920
analysis,

55:17.560 --> 55:18.820
the absolute limits

55:18.820 --> 55:19.600
of looking backward.

55:20.200 --> 55:21.320
Can we actually use

55:21.320 --> 55:22.140
this ancient data

55:22.140 --> 55:23.120
to predict the future?

55:23.560 --> 55:24.100
That's the million

55:24.100 --> 55:24.740
dollar question.

55:25.180 --> 55:26.100
Because if we can't

55:26.100 --> 55:27.420
even definitively

55:27.420 --> 55:28.720
prove mathematically

55:28.720 --> 55:29.740
how the climate

55:29.740 --> 55:31.020
system broke in the

55:31.020 --> 55:31.980
past, whether it was

55:31.980 --> 55:33.440
an excursion or a

55:33.440 --> 55:34.320
true bifurcation,

55:34.320 --> 55:35.680
how can we possibly

55:35.680 --> 55:36.720
justify using this

55:36.720 --> 55:37.560
cave data to

55:37.560 --> 55:38.580
confidently predict

55:38.580 --> 55:39.300
what will happen to

55:39.300 --> 55:40.100
our weather tomorrow?

55:40.600 --> 55:41.500
It is the ultimate

55:41.500 --> 55:42.980
existential challenge

55:42.980 --> 55:44.460
of paleoclimatology.

55:45.580 --> 55:46.600
Flyxion identifies a

55:46.600 --> 55:48.160
massive, often ignored

55:48.160 --> 55:49.340
statistical hurdle here

55:49.340 --> 55:50.800
called causal

55:50.800 --> 55:52.360
non-identifiability.

55:53.200 --> 55:55.380
Causal non-identifiability.

55:56.160 --> 55:57.100
Basically meaning,

55:57.640 --> 55:58.620
we know definitively

55:58.620 --> 55:59.540
that an event happened,

55:59.660 --> 56:00.760
but we lack the ability

56:00.760 --> 56:02.060
to mathematically prove

56:02.060 --> 56:03.340
which specific variable

56:03.340 --> 56:03.960
caused it.

56:04.040 --> 56:04.260
Right.

56:04.960 --> 56:05.660
Think about the sheer

56:05.660 --> 56:06.800
chaos of a glacial

56:06.800 --> 56:07.420
termination.

56:08.180 --> 56:09.360
The entire planetary

56:09.360 --> 56:10.400
ice age is ending.

56:11.120 --> 56:12.100
Basically, every major

56:12.100 --> 56:13.220
climate variable on

56:13.220 --> 56:14.060
Earth is undergoing

56:14.060 --> 56:16.000
massive, violent change

56:16.000 --> 56:17.280
at the exact same

56:17.280 --> 56:18.180
geological moment.

56:18.180 --> 56:19.480
Everything is moving

56:19.480 --> 56:19.960
at once.

56:20.320 --> 56:20.960
The Laurentide ice

56:20.960 --> 56:21.700
sheets are collapsing,

56:22.020 --> 56:23.000
global sea levels are

56:23.000 --> 56:24.160
rising by hundreds of

56:24.160 --> 56:25.720
feet, ocean circulation

56:25.720 --> 56:26.840
patterns are frantically

56:26.840 --> 56:28.600
reorganizing, surface

56:28.600 --> 56:29.760
albedo is dropping,

56:30.420 --> 56:31.780
and atmospheric CO2

56:31.780 --> 56:32.860
is spiking rapidly.

56:33.620 --> 56:34.380
So if you look at the

56:34.380 --> 56:35.460
timeline and you see

56:35.460 --> 56:36.660
the Asian monsoon

56:36.660 --> 56:38.260
completely fail at the

56:38.260 --> 56:39.720
exact moment atmospheric

56:39.720 --> 56:41.780
CO2 spikes, it is

56:41.780 --> 56:43.000
incredibly tempting to

56:43.000 --> 56:43.920
draw a straight line and

56:43.920 --> 56:45.420
say, ah, high CO2

56:45.420 --> 56:46.140
killed the monsoon.

56:46.140 --> 56:47.520
But you cannot make

56:47.520 --> 56:48.520
that causal claim

56:48.520 --> 56:50.160
definitively, because

56:50.160 --> 56:51.380
the catastrophic melting

56:51.380 --> 56:53.260
of the ice sheet was also

56:53.260 --> 56:54.620
happening at that exact

56:54.620 --> 56:56.640
same moment, and the

56:56.640 --> 56:58.840
AMOC was collapsing at

56:58.840 --> 56:59.860
that exact same moment.

57:00.000 --> 57:01.040
You can't isolate the

57:01.040 --> 57:01.420
cause.

57:02.080 --> 57:03.480
Just because CO2 rises

57:03.480 --> 57:04.740
simultaneously with a

57:04.740 --> 57:06.340
monsoon failure does not

57:06.340 --> 57:07.540
mean one mechanically

57:07.540 --> 57:08.860
caused the other.

57:09.480 --> 57:11.280
The temporal coincidence is

57:11.280 --> 57:12.160
actually very weak

57:12.160 --> 57:13.960
evidence, because multiple

57:13.960 --> 57:14.920
different dynamical

57:14.920 --> 57:16.160
mechanisms can generate

57:16.160 --> 57:17.580
observable proxy histories

57:17.580 --> 57:19.240
that look completely

57:19.240 --> 57:20.260
identical to our

57:20.260 --> 57:20.980
limited tools.

57:21.280 --> 57:22.340
Flyxion talks about the

57:22.340 --> 57:24.060
danger of the illusion

57:24.060 --> 57:25.520
of correlation, right?

57:25.920 --> 57:27.040
They bring up a specific

57:27.040 --> 57:28.480
statistical check to prove

57:28.480 --> 57:30.100
this, the Ebizazaki phase

57:30.100 --> 57:31.120
randomization test.

57:31.200 --> 57:32.220
Can we break down how

57:32.220 --> 57:32.640
that works?

57:33.120 --> 57:34.360
The Ebizazaki phase

57:34.360 --> 57:35.680
randomization test is a

57:35.680 --> 57:37.180
brilliant, sobering,

57:37.320 --> 57:38.640
algorithmic reality check

57:38.640 --> 57:39.640
for climate scientists.

57:40.100 --> 57:41.280
When you have two time

57:41.280 --> 57:42.680
series of data that vary,

57:42.840 --> 57:43.960
very slowly over many

57:43.960 --> 57:45.620
thousands of years, like

57:45.620 --> 57:47.380
a slowly warming global

57:47.380 --> 57:48.900
ocean temperature and a

57:48.900 --> 57:50.560
slowly drying cave in

57:50.560 --> 57:52.080
Peru, they can look

57:52.080 --> 57:53.320
highly correlated on a

57:53.320 --> 57:54.580
graph just by pure

57:54.580 --> 57:55.920
statistical chance, not

57:55.920 --> 57:56.340
physics.

57:56.700 --> 57:57.720
Because if you have two

57:57.720 --> 57:59.060
wavy lines that both take

57:59.060 --> 58:00.660
10,000 years to go up and

58:00.660 --> 58:02.000
down, they are bound to

58:02.000 --> 58:03.020
overlap sometimes.

58:03.400 --> 58:03.840
Exactly.

58:04.300 --> 58:05.600
What Ebizazaki's

58:05.600 --> 58:07.620
mathematical method does is

58:07.620 --> 58:08.840
it takes the proxy data,

58:09.380 --> 58:10.820
uses a Fourier transform to

58:10.820 --> 58:11.800
break it down into its

58:11.800 --> 58:13.720
constituent frequencies, and

58:13.720 --> 58:15.160
then literally scrambles the

58:15.160 --> 58:17.160
phase angles, the timing, of

58:17.160 --> 58:18.640
all those waves, while

58:18.640 --> 58:19.900
keeping the overall spectral

58:19.900 --> 58:21.180
power intact.

58:21.700 --> 58:22.660
So it scrambles the data, but

58:22.660 --> 58:23.220
keeps the shape.

58:23.440 --> 58:25.180
It generates thousands of

58:25.180 --> 58:26.440
synthetic timelines that have

58:26.440 --> 58:28.760
the exact same slow rhythm as

58:28.760 --> 58:29.980
the real data, but with

58:29.980 --> 58:30.780
randomized timing.

58:31.240 --> 58:32.480
And then you test your

58:32.480 --> 58:33.460
correlation against the

58:33.460 --> 58:34.040
random data.

58:34.480 --> 58:34.800
Yes.

58:35.180 --> 58:36.700
And very often, you find that

58:36.700 --> 58:37.760
your groundbreaking

58:37.760 --> 58:39.760
correlation between CO2 and

58:39.760 --> 58:41.500
rainfall appears just as

58:41.500 --> 58:42.840
strongly in the completely

58:42.840 --> 58:44.820
randomized fake data.

58:45.500 --> 58:46.220
It proves that the

58:46.220 --> 58:47.480
correlation might just be a

58:47.480 --> 58:49.480
statistical phantom generated

58:49.480 --> 58:50.880
by the slow-moving nature of

58:50.880 --> 58:52.420
the variables, not a true

58:52.420 --> 58:53.340
causal link.

58:54.140 --> 58:55.280
So we have this massive,

58:55.600 --> 58:58.400
incredibly detailed, 450,000-

58:58.400 --> 58:59.780
year cave record, but

58:59.780 --> 59:00.760
Flyxion is essentially

59:00.760 --> 59:02.280
warning us that we can't just

59:02.280 --> 59:03.880
draw straight, simplistic

59:03.880 --> 59:06.000
lines from past causes to

59:06.000 --> 59:06.760
past effects.

59:06.760 --> 59:09.000
We need a much better, more

59:09.000 --> 59:10.400
rigorous plan if we want to

59:10.400 --> 59:11.160
survive the future.

59:11.920 --> 59:13.640
Which is precisely why Flyxion

59:13.640 --> 59:14.820
concludes the essay by

59:14.820 --> 59:17.580
proposing a rigorous, deeply

59:17.580 --> 59:18.840
mathematical, three-step

59:18.840 --> 59:19.780
predictive program.

59:20.640 --> 59:22.900
If we want to use paleoclimate

59:22.900 --> 59:24.280
data to predict our

59:24.280 --> 59:26.680
anthropogenic future, we have to

59:26.680 --> 59:27.540
elevate our standards.

59:28.040 --> 59:29.400
Let's lay out the program.

59:29.700 --> 59:29.920
Okay.

59:30.640 --> 59:31.240
Step one.

59:31.620 --> 59:32.200
Step one.

59:32.400 --> 59:34.200
We must define state-dependent

59:34.200 --> 59:37.040
susceptibility mathematically, not

59:37.040 --> 59:37.840
just descriptively.

59:38.220 --> 59:39.600
We cannot just count how many

59:39.600 --> 59:41.200
times the monsoon failed in the

59:41.200 --> 59:42.900
past and assume a percentage

59:42.900 --> 59:43.420
risk.

59:43.680 --> 59:45.480
We can't just guess based on

59:45.480 --> 59:45.840
history.

59:46.200 --> 59:48.280
We must build dynamical models

59:48.280 --> 59:50.220
that can calculate the exact

59:50.220 --> 59:51.640
derivative of the climate's

59:51.640 --> 59:52.100
response.

59:52.980 --> 59:55.280
We need an equation that tells us

59:55.280 --> 59:57.040
exactly how sensitive the monsoon

59:57.040 --> 59:59.620
is to an AMOC collapse under every

59:59.620 --> 01:00:01.860
possible boundary condition of ice

01:00:01.860 --> 01:00:02.480
and carbon.

01:00:02.480 --> 01:00:04.900
We need the math, not just the

01:00:04.900 --> 01:00:05.240
story.

01:00:05.760 --> 01:00:06.340
Step two.

01:00:06.660 --> 01:00:07.260
Step two.

01:00:07.820 --> 01:00:09.740
We must test all competing climate

01:00:09.740 --> 01:00:11.240
models out of sample.

01:00:11.960 --> 01:00:14.640
This is a huge, often violated rule

01:00:14.640 --> 01:00:15.120
in modeling.

01:00:15.880 --> 01:00:17.800
If a modeling team builds a highly

01:00:17.800 --> 01:00:19.900
complex, parameter-heavy simulation

01:00:19.900 --> 01:00:22.100
that perfectly replicates the monsoon

01:00:22.100 --> 01:00:24.940
failure that occurred 300,000 years ago,

01:00:25.440 --> 01:00:27.600
that is an impressive feat of curve

01:00:27.600 --> 01:00:27.820
fitting.

01:00:27.980 --> 01:00:28.260
Right.

01:00:28.260 --> 01:00:31.200
But the true test of physics is whether

01:00:31.200 --> 01:00:33.360
that exact same model with the exact

01:00:33.360 --> 01:00:35.660
same parameters can accurately predict

01:00:35.660 --> 01:00:37.360
the different monsoon failure that

01:00:37.360 --> 01:00:39.120
happened 100,000 years ago.

01:00:39.660 --> 01:00:41.740
You fit the model on one glacial cycle

01:00:41.740 --> 01:00:43.780
and you force it to test itself on a

01:00:43.780 --> 01:00:45.820
completely different, unseen glacial

01:00:45.820 --> 01:00:47.800
cycle to see if the underlying physics

01:00:47.800 --> 01:00:48.680
actually hold up.

01:00:49.000 --> 01:00:50.880
No cheating by letting the algorithm see

01:00:50.880 --> 01:00:51.740
the answers first.

01:00:52.300 --> 01:00:53.180
And step three.

01:00:53.180 --> 01:00:54.420
Step three.

01:00:54.960 --> 01:00:56.740
We must rigorously propagate

01:00:56.740 --> 01:00:58.400
chronological uncertainty.

01:00:58.800 --> 01:00:59.060
Okay.

01:00:59.240 --> 01:01:00.660
This loops all the way back to the

01:01:00.660 --> 01:01:02.600
uranium-thorium dating we discussed

01:01:02.600 --> 01:01:03.100
at the beginning.

01:01:03.480 --> 01:01:03.880
It does.

01:01:04.540 --> 01:01:06.020
The radiometric dates on those

01:01:06.020 --> 01:01:07.420
stalagmites are incredible,

01:01:07.980 --> 01:01:10.420
but they still carry roughly a 1% error

01:01:10.420 --> 01:01:12.940
margin, which sounds negligible

01:01:12.940 --> 01:01:16.740
until you realize that 1% of 300,000

01:01:16.740 --> 01:01:19.120
years is 3,000 years.

01:01:19.120 --> 01:01:21.860
A 3,000-year margin of error is longer

01:01:21.860 --> 01:01:23.920
than the entire duration of some of

01:01:23.920 --> 01:01:25.360
these extreme drought anomalies.

01:01:25.780 --> 01:01:26.220
Exactly.

01:01:26.820 --> 01:01:28.340
If the true age of the South American

01:01:28.340 --> 01:01:30.940
proxy timeline is actually shifted by

01:01:30.940 --> 01:01:33.400
just 2,000 years within that margin of

01:01:33.400 --> 01:01:35.680
error, while the Asian timeline remains

01:01:35.680 --> 01:01:37.860
where it is, an event that looked

01:01:37.860 --> 01:01:39.640
perfectly synchronous on paper might

01:01:39.640 --> 01:01:41.820
suddenly reveal itself to be a massive,

01:01:42.380 --> 01:01:43.200
staggered delay.

01:01:43.540 --> 01:01:44.100
Wow.

01:01:44.320 --> 01:01:45.320
That changes everything.

01:01:45.960 --> 01:01:48.100
So, Flyxion argues that you cannot just

01:01:48.100 --> 01:01:50.460
run your statistical analysis on the

01:01:50.460 --> 01:01:52.700
single, most likely timeline generated

01:01:52.700 --> 01:01:53.920
by the B-Cron algorithm.

01:01:54.540 --> 01:01:57.180
You must run your model against 10,000

01:01:57.180 --> 01:02:00.500
possible, slightly shifted timelines to

01:02:00.500 --> 01:02:03.000
see if the synchronicity is robust or if

01:02:03.000 --> 01:02:04.920
it vanishes when the dates inevitably

01:02:04.920 --> 01:02:05.340
wiggle.

01:02:05.800 --> 01:02:07.540
It's essentially demanding that the field

01:02:07.540 --> 01:02:10.000
stops treating its proxy timelines as

01:02:10.000 --> 01:02:12.680
absolute gospel truth and starts treating

01:02:12.680 --> 01:02:15.200
them as statistical probability distributions.

01:02:15.560 --> 01:02:16.080
Precisely.

01:02:16.080 --> 01:02:18.620
Because if we don't hold ourselves to these

01:02:18.620 --> 01:02:20.500
punishingly strict mathematical standards,

01:02:20.680 --> 01:02:23.000
we risk drawing entirely the wrong

01:02:23.000 --> 01:02:25.240
conclusions about our own immediate future.

01:02:25.860 --> 01:02:28.020
Which brings us to the ultimate

01:02:28.020 --> 01:02:30.040
inescapable reality check,

01:02:30.740 --> 01:02:32.420
the anthropogenic contrast.

01:02:32.420 --> 01:02:35.280
And this is exactly where the rubber meets

01:02:35.280 --> 01:02:37.280
the road for anyone listening right now.

01:02:37.580 --> 01:02:39.240
If you're listening to this, you are

01:02:39.240 --> 01:02:40.980
probably trying to figure out what this

01:02:40.980 --> 01:02:44.120
450,000-year-old rock means for your

01:02:44.120 --> 01:02:46.640
weather next year, or your children's

01:02:46.640 --> 01:02:47.500
weather in 50 years.

01:02:47.980 --> 01:02:50.180
You want to know if the AMOC is going to

01:02:50.180 --> 01:02:52.360
collapse tomorrow and completely turn off

01:02:52.360 --> 01:02:53.140
the rain in the Amazon.

01:02:53.780 --> 01:02:55.760
But Flyxion points out a glaring issue.

01:02:56.500 --> 01:02:58.820
The past is a foreign country, and its

01:02:58.820 --> 01:03:00.040
geography doesn't match ours.

01:03:00.040 --> 01:03:02.720
The massive, synchronous failures of the

01:03:02.720 --> 01:03:04.500
global rain system recorded in the caves,

01:03:05.020 --> 01:03:07.120
these terrifying glitches in the matrix,

01:03:07.720 --> 01:03:10.000
happened during natural glacial terminations.

01:03:10.740 --> 01:03:12.440
Those were periods characterized by the

01:03:12.440 --> 01:03:14.520
melting of massive, continent-spanning

01:03:14.520 --> 01:03:16.840
ice sheets in North America and Eurasia.

01:03:17.400 --> 01:03:18.880
And that meltwater was the trigger.

01:03:19.580 --> 01:03:21.940
The sheer volume of fresh water locked in

01:03:21.940 --> 01:03:24.720
those specific geographic locations was the

01:03:24.720 --> 01:03:27.780
massive hammer that struck the ocean currents.

01:03:27.780 --> 01:03:30.120
But look at the Earth today.

01:03:30.860 --> 01:03:33.200
We are warming the planet at a terrifyingly

01:03:33.200 --> 01:03:36.300
rapid, unnatural rate due to human-made

01:03:36.300 --> 01:03:37.420
greenhouse gas emissions.

01:03:37.840 --> 01:03:40.040
But we fundamentally lack those massive

01:03:40.040 --> 01:03:41.920
Laurentide glacial ice sheets to melt.

01:03:42.320 --> 01:03:44.380
We have the Greenland ice sheet, and we have

01:03:44.380 --> 01:03:45.400
Antarctica, yes.

01:03:45.580 --> 01:03:48.220
But the total global ice volume, and

01:03:48.220 --> 01:03:50.500
specifically its geographic distribution in

01:03:50.500 --> 01:03:53.280
relation to the Atlantic, is historically

01:03:53.280 --> 01:03:55.660
incredibly low compared to the start of a

01:03:55.660 --> 01:03:56.580
glacial termination.

01:03:57.040 --> 01:03:59.280
Which means that the boundary conditions of

01:03:59.280 --> 01:04:00.960
our planet right this second are

01:04:00.960 --> 01:04:04.040
fundamentally, structurally different from

01:04:04.040 --> 01:04:05.840
the boundary conditions that triggered the

01:04:05.840 --> 01:04:08.000
anomalies in the past 450,000 years.

01:04:08.280 --> 01:04:10.860
We are missing the primary weapon that the

01:04:10.860 --> 01:04:12.640
climate used to break the seesaw.

01:04:13.500 --> 01:04:15.920
Flyxion's thesis really drives this home, and

01:04:15.920 --> 01:04:17.100
it's a brilliant way to frame it.

01:04:17.640 --> 01:04:19.660
The persistence of a historical correlation

01:04:19.660 --> 01:04:22.880
correlation is not an unconditional law of

01:04:22.880 --> 01:04:23.220
physics.

01:04:23.980 --> 01:04:25.580
Just because the climate responded to

01:04:25.580 --> 01:04:28.140
warming in a very specific way 200,000

01:04:28.140 --> 01:04:30.420
years ago does not guarantee it will

01:04:30.420 --> 01:04:32.740
respond the exact same way tomorrow.

01:04:33.320 --> 01:04:34.940
Because the background state of the planet

01:04:34.940 --> 01:04:36.740
has radically shifted.

01:04:37.280 --> 01:04:39.000
The stage is set differently.

01:04:39.780 --> 01:04:41.320
The forcing can continue to act.

01:04:41.920 --> 01:04:43.680
The orbital wobble is still happening.

01:04:43.820 --> 01:04:44.780
CO2 is still rising.

01:04:44.780 --> 01:04:46.920
But if the boundary conditions are

01:04:46.920 --> 01:04:49.100
different, the emergent response can

01:04:49.100 --> 01:04:51.220
completely, unpredictably change.

01:04:51.720 --> 01:04:53.780
And that reality is both the beauty and

01:04:54.440 --> 01:04:56.920
the sheer terror of state-dependent coupling.

01:04:57.300 --> 01:05:00.020
We cannot simply copy-paste the paleo-climate

01:05:00.020 --> 01:05:01.060
record onto our future.

01:05:01.620 --> 01:05:03.960
It is an invaluable guide to the physics, but

01:05:03.960 --> 01:05:06.380
it is not a literal script for the 21st

01:05:06.380 --> 01:05:06.720
century.

01:05:06.840 --> 01:05:07.180
Not at all.

01:05:07.700 --> 01:05:09.860
So as we pull all of these threads

01:05:09.860 --> 01:05:11.700
together, let's summarize the immense

01:05:11.700 --> 01:05:12.680
ground we've covered today.

01:05:12.680 --> 01:05:16.020
The orbital seesaw, the interhemispheric

01:05:16.020 --> 01:05:17.920
dipole that has governed global rainfall

01:05:17.920 --> 01:05:19.680
for eons, is conditional.

01:05:20.420 --> 01:05:23.180
It is an emergent pattern, not an invariant

01:05:23.180 --> 01:05:24.540
unbreakable law of physics.

01:05:25.220 --> 01:05:27.460
A stack of stalagmites sitting in a damp

01:05:27.460 --> 01:05:29.880
Peruvian cave decisively proves that our

01:05:29.880 --> 01:05:32.060
climate's rules can be temporarily rewritten

01:05:32.060 --> 01:05:34.220
when the planet's background conditions

01:05:34.220 --> 01:05:36.680
undergo massive thermodynamic stress.

01:05:37.260 --> 01:05:38.720
The master volume can drop.

01:05:39.120 --> 01:05:39.380
Right.

01:05:40.120 --> 01:05:41.920
The massive baseline shift of the common

01:05:41.920 --> 01:05:44.120
mode can completely drown out the steady

01:05:44.120 --> 01:05:45.360
rhythm of the difference mode.

01:05:45.980 --> 01:05:47.860
The foundational physical laws of moisture

01:05:47.860 --> 01:05:50.120
conservation remain perfectly intact globally,

01:05:50.420 --> 01:05:53.120
but the geographical distribution of that

01:05:53.120 --> 01:05:55.880
moisture is terrifyingly sensitive to the state

01:05:55.880 --> 01:05:58.540
of the oceans, the volume of the ice, and the

01:05:58.540 --> 01:05:59.720
geometry of the atmosphere.

01:06:00.260 --> 01:06:02.760
Which leaves us with a final, lingering, and

01:06:02.760 --> 01:06:04.500
frankly provocative thought for you to

01:06:04.500 --> 01:06:06.660
mill over as you look at the weather today.

01:06:06.660 --> 01:06:10.400
If the rules of the planetary monsoon seesaw were

01:06:10.400 --> 01:06:14.000
temporarily suspended in the ancient past, strictly

01:06:14.000 --> 01:06:18.260
due to changing amounts of ice and CO2, what does it mean for

01:06:18.260 --> 01:06:18.800
us today?

01:06:19.360 --> 01:06:23.180
Our current boundary conditions, this highly volatile

01:06:23.180 --> 01:06:26.940
combination of rapidly rising anthropogenic greenhouse

01:06:26.940 --> 01:06:30.820
gases combined with a historically low amount of global ice,

01:06:30.820 --> 01:06:37.080
have arguably never existed in the entire 450,000-year stalagmite record.

01:06:37.580 --> 01:06:39.280
We are completely off the map.

01:06:39.280 --> 01:06:43.680
It is entirely unprecedented mathematical and thermodynamic territory.

01:06:43.680 --> 01:06:47.520
If our current boundary conditions are completely unprecedented,

01:06:48.140 --> 01:06:51.300
then perhaps the future of our climate isn't just going to be a simple

01:06:51.300 --> 01:06:53.340
glitch in the old rules.

01:06:53.860 --> 01:06:56.420
It's not just the seesaw breaking and dropping the rain.

01:06:56.740 --> 01:06:59.240
We might be entering an entirely new game,

01:06:59.520 --> 01:07:03.500
where the ancient paleoclimate record can no longer serve as a reliable map.

01:07:03.900 --> 01:07:05.300
The seesaw hasn't just snapped.

01:07:05.740 --> 01:07:08.840
Through our emissions, we might have built a completely different,

01:07:09.040 --> 01:07:10.600
unpredictable machine in its place.

01:07:10.600 --> 01:07:15.160
It is a sobering thought, and it strongly reinforces Flyxion's demand

01:07:15.160 --> 01:07:19.380
that we build better, mathematically flawless, predictive, dynamical models,

01:07:19.780 --> 01:07:23.160
rather than just looking backward into the proxy record for false comfort.

01:07:23.720 --> 01:07:25.060
We have to do the math.

01:07:25.880 --> 01:07:27.380
We have to do the math.

01:07:28.140 --> 01:07:31.320
Thank you so much for joining us on this deep dive into the physical limits

01:07:31.320 --> 01:07:32.420
of our climate's past.

01:07:32.880 --> 01:07:34.140
We will catch you next time.

